@meta
id: 20-04
title: Two registers, one swap
chapter: 20
type: lesson
skills: register-named
par: 50
minutes: 5
vscode: select one, cut, select the other, paste, find the first again, paste
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Named registers earn their keep when you need to hold two things at
once. Swapping is the obvious case: with a single register you have to
put one thing down before you can pick the other up, and the dance of
doing that in the right order is where mistakes creep in. With two
registers there is no dance. Pick both up, then put each where the other
was.

`settle` calls `this.paymentService.capture(total, orderId)`. The
service's signature is `capture(orderId, amount)`. The arguments are
the wrong way round. Swap them.

@keys
| `"ayiw` | yank the word under the cursor into `a` |
| `"byiw` | and the next one into `b` |
| `diw` then `"aP` | delete a word, put `a` where it was |
| `F(` | back to the previous `(` on the line |

@hints
Yank `total` into one register and `orderId` into another. Then each
word is replaced with `diw` followed by a put from the *other* register.
--
`diw` leaves the cursor on the character after the gap; `"aP` puts
before it, which is exactly where the word was.
--
`/capture(` Enter, `f(` `l`, `"ayiw`, `2w`, `"byiw`, `diw` `"aP`,
`F(` `l`, `diw` `"bP`.

@solution
    /capture(<Enter> f(l "ayiw 2w "byiw diw"aP F(l diw"bP :wq<Enter>

Both words are in hand before anything is deleted, so the two `diw`
calls can trample the unnamed register as much as they like. That is
the whole reason named registers exist: you decide what is precious,
give it a letter, and stop worrying about the order of everything else.

There is a one-register version of this swap, and it works by knowing
that `diw` itself fills the unnamed register. It is shorter and it is
fragile. Learn the two-register version first. When you can see the
shortcut yourself, take it.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async settle(orderId: string, total: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(orderId);

    if (order.status !== 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.capture(total, orderId);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: orderId },
      values: { status: 'SETTLED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async settle(orderId: string, total: number) {
    const order = await this.findOne(orderId);

    if (order.status !== 'SHIPPED') {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_SHIPPED');
    }

    await this.paymentService.capture(orderId, total);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id: orderId },
      values: { status: 'SETTLED' },
    });
  }
}
